| NIST warns that hashing/encryption transformations can create re-identification risk; release of a key or procedural error can compromise identities across a dataset. | NIST SP 800-188, De-Identifying Government Datasets | NIST | 2022; accessed 2026-09-11 | [NIST](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-188.pdf) | T1 | Discussion of frequency attacks and transformation/key risks | Do not label salted hashes “irreversible” without a documented threat model. Treat them as pseudonymous transforms whose risk depends on input entropy, key/salt exposure, and linkage data. | high |
| A unique salt prevents precomputed lookup tables but does not by itself make fast hashing appropriate for password protection. | Password Storage Cheat Sheet | OWASP | undated; accessed 2026-09-11 | [OWASP](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html) | T1 | Salting section | The analogy is limited: join-preserving identifiers are not passwords. Add warnings for deterministic or reusable transforms, low-entropy values, and salt-table exposure. | high |
